  "worked_solution": "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ -3x\\end{aligned}],\\,x\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of the zero-vector satisfy the equation $y=-3x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x \\\\ (0) & =-3(0) \\\\ 0 & =0\\,\u2713\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ -3a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ -3b\\end{aligned}]\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ -3a\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ -3b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a+b \\\\ (-3a)+(-3b)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a+b \\\\ -3(a+b)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $y=-3x.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ -3a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ -3a\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}ka \\\\ k(-3a)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}ka \\\\ -3(ka)\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $y=-3x.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are true, $L$ must be a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\"",
